      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wearing Hanfu</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Hanfu has multiple layers, and the order matters. The shop assistant will help you put on the inner garment (zhongyi) first — a lightweight cotton or silk layer worn against the skin. Next comes the main robe (usually a cross-collar ao or a round-collar yuanlingpao), followed by the outer layer or coat. The sash or belt is tied at the waist, and hairstyling completes the look. For women, the process takes about 20-30 minutes; for men, about 15 minutes. Expect to spend 30-45 minutes total from selection to finished styling. Pro tip: wear a thin, neutral-colored top underneath so the inner garment sits smoothly, and avoid bulky clothing that shows through the fabric.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wearing Qipao</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Qipao is straightforward by comparison — it''s essentially a one-piece dress. The key is fit. A well-fitted qipao should be snug but not restrictive, with the side slits reaching no higher than mid-thigh. The mandarin collar sits close to the neck without choking. Rental shops will have you try 2-3 sizes. For custom tailoring in Shanghai''s South Bund Fabric Market, your measurements are taken at the first visit, and you return 3-5 days later for a final fitting. Wear seamless undergarments and bring the shoes you plan to pair with the dress to the fitting.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wearing Tang Suit & Zhongshan Suit</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">The Tang suit is a jacket-and-stand-collar combination that goes over your own shirt. Most shops provide the jacket; you wear your own trousers. The Zhongshan suit is a full tailored outfit with four patch pockets and a turned-down collar — closer to Western suiting in how it''s worn. Both styles take about 10 minutes to put on and adjust. For custom orders, the tailor will take chest, shoulder, sleeve, and waist measurements. Turnaround in Beijing hutong tailors is typically 2-4 days.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to Wear Underneath</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">For Hanfu: a thin tank top or camisole and fitted shorts or leggings. The rental shop provides the inner garment, but having your own base layer adds comfort, especially in summer. For qipao: seamless underwear is essential — the form-fitting silhouette shows every line. Shapewear is commonly worn by locals. For Tang and Zhongshan suits: a plain crew-neck t-shirt and slim-fit trousers work perfectly. Avoid collared shirts under Tang suits since the mandarin collar already frames the neck.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Etiquette & Tips</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Walk carefully in long robes — Hanfu hems can drag, especially on stairs and uneven stone paths common at historical sites. For photoshoots: hold the sleeves gracefully when raising your arms; let the fabric drape naturally. When sitting in qipao, cross your ankles rather than your legs to keep the side slits modest. Most importantly: relax. The shop assistants have dressed thousands of people. They know what looks good, and they want you to have a great experience. A smile and basic "xie xie" (thank you) go a long way.</p>
